<h2>3. Length-scale as model complexity</h2>

<p>
Short length-scales let nearby observations vary independently; long length-scales
force the function to move as a broad sheet. The same data can look overfit,
reasonable, or underfit depending on $\ell$.
</p>

<Figure title="Figure 3 · Same observations under three length-scales" canvasId="fig3" width="760" height="340" readoutId="fig3-readout" />

<h2>4. Marginal likelihood landscape</h2>

<p>
The log marginal likelihood scores hyperparameters by integrating out the latent
function:
</p>

$$
\log p(y\mid X,\theta)=
-\frac12 y^T(K_\theta+\sigma_n^2I)^&#123;-1&#125;y
-\frac12\log|K_\theta+\sigma_n^2I|
-\frac n2\log(2\pi).
$$

<p>
Click the landscape to update the posterior below it. The optimum balances fit,
uncertainty, and complexity.
</p>

<Figure title="Figure 4 · Hyperparameter learning by marginal likelihood" canvasId="fig4" width="760" height="470" readoutId="fig4-readout" />

<Figure title="Figure 4b · Same data, different kernels, different assumptions" canvasId="fig4-kernels" width="760" height="420" readoutId="fig4-kernels-readout">
