<h2>5. Two-dimensional regression toy</h2>

<p>
The same conditioning formula works over any input space. In two dimensions, the
posterior mean becomes a surface. The heatmap below draws that mean; opacity fades
where posterior uncertainty is high. Drag training points to reshape the surface.
</p>

<Figure title="Figure 5 · 2-D posterior mean with uncertainty transparency" canvasId="fig5" width="760" height="420" readoutId="fig5-readout" />

<h2>6. Acquisition functions select the next point</h2>

<p>
Bayesian optimization uses the GP posterior to choose where to evaluate next. Expected
improvement is high where the mean is promising, the uncertainty is large, or both.
</p>

<Figure title="Figure 6 · Expected improvement under the current posterior" canvasId="fig6" width="760" height="330" readoutId="fig6-readout">
<Fragment slot="legend">
